# A named law's `for` lines are its binders, `law doubled:` then `for n: nat` is `law doubled(n: nat): …`: laws are
# part of the language, so every program and library file reads them, and each build's own law rules after them
# (extensions/laws.wap, law_runs.wap, law_statements.wap)
rule law ($name: {for $binder {$type}; $lines...}) => law_for $name [$binder: $type] {$lines...}
rule law_for $name [$binders...] {for $binder {$type}; $lines...} => law_for $name [$binders..., $binder: $type] {$lines...}
rule law_for $name [$binders...] {$statement} => law($name: (($binders...) -> $statement))
# A law or a theorem with a proof, in every build, is the function giving the proof (wiki/proof.md), which a program
# may call: its result the statement, read as its truth, its body the proof the kernel checks (core/analyze/terms.wap),
# claimed to do nothing but compute its value, so a proof that may loop, raise or act is refused by the claim
# (core/analyze/effects.wap claims_checked), at its letters too (@proof, core/analyze/types.wap typed_alone)
# DONE (card rule-word-set, theorem → law below) one rule each `where $word in [theorem, law]` once a rule's where takes more than one word
# The proof is taken off the head first, as the head and the proof a definition is built of, so a law a rule writes
# meets these as one the program wrote does; a theorem is a law that must be proved
rule theorem($head := $proof) => law($head := $proof)
rule law($head := $proof) => law_proof $proof ($head)
rule law_proof $proof ($name($parameters...): $statement) => (@proof $name($parameters...): $statement := $proof ! Pure)
rule law_proof $proof ($name: $statement) => (@proof $name(): $statement := $proof ! Pure)
